 "limits": [
  "The simulator has no seasonality. The history is compared through year-over-year growth, which removes it there; nothing is tested about seasonal quarters.",
  "The fuel volatility window is short and recent: the 65 trading days before the cutoff (13 April 2026 to 16 July 2026), the quarter of the fuel shock. One quarter of daily prices sets most of the width measured here, and it is not changed here.",
  "Few and overlapping outcomes: 33 check outcomes come from 12 quarters and 63 earlier outcomes from 22, so the counts are not independent. 76% against a nominal 80% is a difference of 1.4 outcomes.",
  "The assumed value was kept because of its coverage on 2022-2026, so that figure is the reason for the choice, not an independent test of it.",
  "The assumed value is too wide for 2013-2019 (63 of 63 inside). Unit revenue was 2.5 times as variable in 2022-2026 as in 2013-2019 two quarters out (root mean square 8.9% against 3.5%), so no single value fits both periods.",
  "The rule keeps 2022Q1, a quarter still held down by the pandemic. Without it the check years hold 25 of 30 (83%) unit-revenue outcomes at the assumed value and 20 of 30 (67%) at the fitted one, and 28 of 30 (93%) margin outcomes. That count is a sensitivity, not the check.",
